Title IX Office\nRequest Info\nSearch\nCommon Search Terms:\nadmissions,\napply now,\nonline learning,\nrequest information,\nschedule of courses,\nacademic calendar,\nbert,\nregistrar,\nlibrary,\nhuman resources,\nhousing,\nhr,\nwork order,\nregistration,\njobs,\nmubert\nGraduate Studies\nHome\nPrograms\nProspective students\nAdmissions\nAdmissions Contacts\nInternational Applicants\nCosts and Aid\nGraduate Assistantship Information\nGraduate Admission Exams\nCurrent Students\nInfo for students\nGraduate Advisor Directory\nAcademic Appeals\nStudent support services\nForms and Information\nTheses and Dissertations\nETD Info\nETD Guidelines\nETD Training\nDeadlines & Dates\nGraduation Timeline\nUniversity Calendar\nFaculty\nGraduate Council\nCurriculum Forms\nRedBook (Graduate Faculty List)\nGraduate Faculty Requirements\nGraduate Admission Exams\nGRE\nLSAT\nPraxis\nThe Marshall University Testing Center administers a number of exams which are required for admission to graduate and/or professional schools. These exams are provided nationwide by organizations such as American College Testing (ACT) and the Educational Testing Service (ETS).\nMarshall University Testing Center\nMorrow Library-G45\n304-696-2604\nMarshall University is a test center for the GRE, the PRAXIS, and the MAT (see below). Registration and scoring for the GRE and the PRAXIS is handled by the national testing agencies.\nFor information about the various graduate and professional school entrance exams, registration, scoring, test dates, practice items,etc., please see below.\nGRE\nThe GRE (Graduate Record Examination) is a national testing program, administered by ETS, and is required for admission into a variety of graduate programs.\nThe GRE\u00ae Revised General Test measures verbal reasoning, quantitative reasoning, critical thinking, and analytical writing skills that have been acquired over a long period of time and that are not related to any specific field of study.\nMarshall University is a test center for the GRE. For test days and times at the Marshall University Testing Center call 304-696-2604.\nFor complete information about the GRE, including registration information, go to the GRE Website, and also see the GRE practice tools.\nLSAT\nThe Law School Admission Test (LSAT) is a half-day standardized test required for admission to all ABA-approved law schools and many non-ABA-approved law schools. The test provides a standard measure of acquired reading and verbal reasoning skills that law schools can use as one of several factors in assessing applicants. The test is administered four times a year at hundreds of locations around the world.\nFor more detailed information about test preparation and registration please see Marshall University\u2019s Pre-Law Advisor:\nPatricia Proctor, Director, Simon Perry Center\nOld Main 230L\n304-696-801\nproctor14@marshall.edu\nFor complete information, including registration information, please see the LSAC website.\nPraxis\nThe Praxis Test Series (Professional Assessments for Beginning Teachers) is administered nationwide by ETS. Marshall University is a test center for the Praxis Examinations.\nFor test days and times at the Marshall University Testing Center for the Praxis I (Individual Test Sections) and the Praxis I \u2013 Combined, call 304-696-2604.\nFor additional information, including how to register for the Praxis, see the Praxis website.\nOther Graduate and Professional Entrance Exams\nInformation about the Graduate Management Admissions Test (GMAT)\nInformation about the Medical College Admissions Test (MCAT)\nInformation about the Pharmacy College Admissions Test (PCAT)\nContact Us\nGraduate Studies Office\nOld Main 359\ngraduatestudies@marshall.edu\n304-696-2975\nGraduate Studies Contacts\nGraduate Admissions\ngraduateadmissions@marshall.edu\n304-696-4723\n304-696-4723 ext-61900\nGraduate Admissions Contacts\nProgram Directors\nList of Graduate Program Directors\nLooking for a form?\nIf you do not 